  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A significant system begins to impact the \nregion today with flash flooding and severe weather all on the \ntable. The flooding threat will continue into Wednesday. A \nFlood Watch is in place for most of New Jersey, all of eastern \nPennsylvania, and northern Delaware. \n\nOnly one key message in the discussion this morning but impacts\nare expected to be felt region-wide and over multiple days. The\nmost significant and impactful day will be today though as a\ndeepening area of low pressure moves toward New York, with a\nwarm front lifting through today and a strong cold front coming\nin tonight. The front, aided by strong jet dynamics, will\nprovide ample lift for widespread showers and thunderstorms and\nperhaps multiple rounds today and into tonight. \n\nWe will break out the hazards and threats by starting with the\nflash flooding threat over the next two days. A lifting warm\nfront will only strengthen southerly flow through today,\nushering in a very moist airmass. PWATs will approach two \ninches which is over the 90th percentile even for late July. A \nwarm cloud layer over 10k feet will also aid in producing very \nefficient rainfall rates. Rates of 1-3\" per hour are expected \nlater this afternoon and evening. This, combined with the \npotential for multiple rounds of precipitation will result in a \npretty significant flash flood threat. The WPC Excessive \nRainfall Outlook reflects that, having a MODERATE (3/4) Risk \nfor Excessive Rainfall for Northern New Jersey with a SLIGHT\n(2/4) Risk extending through the rest of the region. The biggest\nconcern will be the more urban areas which begin to see flash \nflooding generally around 1\" per hour and that seems very likely\nconsidering today's setup especially in the afternoon and \nevening. Some areas may also be more flood prone, given \nyesterday's rainfall where there were some flooding reports. \n\nNo significant changes in the Flood Watch, which remains in\nplace for most of New Jersey, all of eastern Pennsylvania, and \nnorthern Delaware. Lower rainfall amounts and areas more in need\nof rainfall over far South Jersey and most of Delmarva will \nresult in a lesser flood threat and those areas staying outside \nof the watch. Cannot rule out flash flooding entirely though in \nthese areas (as we saw yesterday in localized spots) and most of\nSouth Jersey and Delmarva remains in either a Marginal (1/4) or\nSlight (2/4) Risk for Excessive Rainfall. \n\nThe one minor change was extending the Flood Watch north of I-78\nthrough Wednesday. As low pressure meanders, another front will\ncome through on Wednesday, capable of producing some heavy\ndownpours yet again for the northern third of the region. This\nwill move over some more urbanized areas in North Jersey that \nshould get a decent amount of rainfall today, potentially \ncausing more problems. While mainstem river flooding is not \ncurrently forecast, some smaller creeks and streams within the \nPassaic and Raritan basins could see some rises that result in \nflooding. \n\nWhen all is said and done, the current QPF forecast through\nWednesday has around 2 to 5 inches of rain north of I-78, with 1\nto 3 inches elsewhere. As is usual in summer convective setups,\nlocally higher amounts are possible. HREF Max in northern New\nJersey has highly localized amounts up to 8\" through Wednesday.\nHave multiple ways to receive any flood alerts. \n\nIn terms of the severe threat, no significant changes were made\nto the forecast. Unlike the hydro threat, the severe threat will\nbe more focused on the southern half of the area. SPC did nudge\nthe Slight Risk (2/5) a touch south, having far southeast PA,\nSouth Jersey, and all of Delmarva in. The Marginal Risk (1/5) is\nin place from Philadelphia on north. Instability will increase\nquickly once the warm front passes. Delmarva and South Jersey\nwill have more time in the warm sector and have higher amounts\nof shear to work with. The limiting factor further north towards\nPhilly and points north will be the amount of cloud cover. Main\ntiming window is from 2 PM through about 11 PM and damaging \nwind gusts the primary threat. Cannot rule out a tornado, though\nthe tornado threat will be more focused over Delmarva. No area \nis currently in any severe outlook category for Wednesday. \n\nGuidance continues to have the surface low to the north\npotentially lingering through Thursday, though impacts should\nmainly be confined through tomorrow. High pressure nudges in\ntowards the end of the week and for the weekend, giving us a\nbreak from the active pattern.",
